If `nPrecision` (parsed from the ATTDESC finter, after the comma, e.g., "R6,3" means precision 3) is greater than `nWidth` (length of raw value), then `nWidth - nPrecision` becomes negative. When passed as `size_t` to strncpy, this becomes a huge number, causing a crash (buffer overflow / segfault reading way beyond).

Actually wait, looking at it more carefully: nWidth - nPrecision is negative. strncpy with huge n copies strlen(src)+1 up to n. Hmm, actually strncpy copies at most n bytes, padding with zeros. Since src is small, it copies the full string then pads with zeros for the remainder of n - writing to a 30-byte buffer for ~4 billion bytes = segfault. That is the crash.

Actually wait — if nWidth - nPrecision < 0, then as size_t it's huge (like 4294967294). strncpy would copy strlen(pszRawValue) bytes, then zero-pad until n total bytes are written to szRealString (30 bytes buffer). That will definitely overflow and crash.

